<h1>Points vs Cash Calculator</h1>
<p class="sub">Compare ticket cash price against miles required, award taxes, and the value of forgone credit card rewards to determine whether you should pay cash or redeem points.</p>

<div class="direct-answer">
  <strong>Direct Decision Rule:</strong> Compare the net cash cost saved with the value of points required after taxes, fees, and forgone credit card rewards. If the calculated Cents Per Point (CPP) exceeds your baseline valuation for that currency, redeeming points offers better mathematical value.
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <div class="field">
    <label for="cashPrice">Cash ticket price<span class="hint" id="hintCash">$</span></label>
    <input type="number" id="cashPrice" value="500" min="0" step="1" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="hintCash">
  </div>
  
  <div class="row">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="pointsNeeded">Points or miles required<span class="hint" id="hintPoints">Points</span></label>
      <input type="number" id="pointsNeeded" value="40000" min="1" step="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-describedby="hintPoints">
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <label for="awardTaxes">Award taxes and surcharges<span class="hint" id="hintTaxes">$</span></label>
      <input type="number" id="awardTaxes" value="50" min="0" step="1" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="hintTaxes">
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="row">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="forgoneValue">Value of forgone points<span class="hint" id="hintForgone">$ (Optional)</span></label>
      <input type="number" id="forgoneValue" value="20" min="0" step="1" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="hintForgone unitForgone">
      <div class="unit" id="unitForgone">The value of points you would earn if you bought the cash ticket</div>
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <label for="transferBonus">Transfer bonus<span class="hint" id="hintBonus">% (Optional)</span></label>
      <input type="number" id="transferBonus" value="0" min="0" step="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-describedby="hintBonus unitBonus">
      <div class="unit" id="unitBonus">E.g., enter 20 for a 20% transfer bonus</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="personalValuation">Personal point valuation<span class="hint" id="hintValuation">¢ / Point (Optional)</span></label>
    <input type="number" id="personalValuation" value="1.5" min="0" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="hintValuation unitValuation">
    <div class="unit" id="unitValuation">Your baseline expectation for how much a point is worth</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="ticket" aria-live="polite">
  <div class="main">
    <div class="compare">
      <div class="item">
        <div class="k">Redemption Value (CPP)</div>
        <div class="v" id="cppResult" style="word-break: break-word; overflow-wrap: anywhere;">0.00¢ / point</div>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <div class="k">Equivalent Cost</div>
        <div class="v" id="costCompare" style="font-size:18px; margin-top:10px; word-break: break-word; overflow-wrap: anywhere;">Points Cost: -<br>Cash Cost: -</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="note" id="explain"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="perf"></div>
  <div class="stub">
    <div>
      <div class="verdict-label">Recommendation</div>
      <div class="verdict" id="verdictText">—</div>
    </div>
    <div class="code" id="verdictCode">DIFF —</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="article-content prose" style="margin-top: 40px; padding-top: 40px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
  <h2>The Math Behind the Calculator</h2>
<p>Our advanced calculator uses the following formula to determine the actual Cents Per Point (CPP) after accounting for all fees and forgone rewards:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  <span class="formula-name">Actual CPP =</span>
  <span class="formula-expression">[(Cash Price - Award Taxes - Forgone Rewards) ÷ Required Points] × 100</span>
</div>
<p>It then compares this Actual CPP against your Personal Valuation. If Actual CPP > Personal Valuation, using points is mathematically optimal.</p>

<h3>Real-World Example</h3>
<div class="example-box">
  <h4>Example: The Hidden Cost of Forgone Rewards</h4>
  <p>A flight costs $500 cash or 40,000 points + $50 in taxes. If you pay cash using a travel card that earns 3x points, you would earn 1,500 points (worth ~$30). The "Forgone Rewards" is $30.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Net Cash Saved</strong>: $500 (Cash Price) - $50 (Taxes) - $30 (Forgone) = $420</li>
    <li><strong>Actual CPP</strong>: ($420 ÷ 40,000) × 100 = 1.05¢ / point</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If your personal baseline valuation for these points is 1.5¢, the calculator will recommend <strong>Paying Cash</strong> because 1.05¢ < 1.5¢.</p>
</div>

<h2>The 5-Step Award vs. Cash Decision Framework</h2>
  <div class="decision-framework">
    <h3>Systematic Evaluation Process</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Calculate Actual Net CPP</strong>: Subtract mandatory award taxes and forgone cash ticket rewards from the retail cash price, then divide by the net points required.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare with Baseline Valuation</strong>: Benchmark the result against standard redemption targets (e.g. 1.2¢–1.5¢ per airline mile or 0.6¢–0.8¢ per hotel point).</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluate Out-of-Pocket Surcharges</strong>: High fuel surcharges ($300–$800+) on certain international carriers can substantially erode points efficiency.</li>
      <li><strong>Assess Expiration and Transfer Rules</strong>: Prioritize burning miles that face expiration or belong to single-airline programs over flexible bank points.</li>
      <li><strong>Align with Personal Cash-Flow</strong>: If preserving liquid cash is an immediate personal priority, redeeming points at an acceptable baseline value can still be rational.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Representative Decision Scenarios</h2>
  <p>The following examples illustrate how different ticket structures affect the math:</p>

  <div class="case-card">
    <h4>Scenario 1: Domestic Economy Flight (Low Taxes, Moderate Value)</h4>
    <p>A domestic economy ticket costs <strong>$350 cash</strong> versus <strong>25,000 miles + $30 in taxes</strong>. If paying cash would have yielded $15 in rewards, the net cash saved is $305 ($350 - $30 - $15). Realized value is <strong>1.22¢ / point</strong> ($305 ÷ 25,000). If your personal threshold for this airline is 1.2¢, redeeming points is slightly favorable or a balanced toss-up.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="case-card">
    <h4>Scenario 2: Long-Haul Business Class (High Retail Price, High Surcharges)</h4>
    <p>An international business class seat lists for <strong>$3,800 cash</strong> versus <strong>80,000 miles + $650 in taxes & fuel surcharges</strong>. Assuming $70 forgone cash-ticket rewards, net cash saved is $3,080. Realized value is <strong>3.85¢ / point</strong> ($3,080 ÷ 80,000). Despite paying $650 out-of-pocket, the return per point substantially exceeds standard benchmarks.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Understanding "Break-Even CPP"</h2>
  <p>Break-even CPP is the minimum value per point where the financial cost of redeeming equals the net cash price of paying out-of-pocket. When a redemption yields a CPP above your personal break-even valuation, you save more net money by using miles. When it falls below, paying cash and conserving your points for future premium flights is the financially disciplined strategy.</p>

  <h2>What This Calculator Does Not Evaluate</h2>
  <p>While this tool provides rigorous mathematical guidance, award travel decisions also involve non-financial constraints that you should evaluate independently:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Award Seat Availability</strong>: Award space fluctuates dynamically and may not be available on your target flight.</li>
    <li><strong>Ticket Flexibility & Change Policies</strong>: Award tickets often offer cheaper cancellation or change fees than non-refundable basic economy cash fares.</li>
    <li><strong>Cabin Experience & Product Quality</strong>: Mathematical valuation does not measure seat comfort, lounge access, or personal convenience.</li>
    <li><strong>Individual Liquidity Needs</strong>: Personal budgeting priorities and short-term cash flow needs take precedence over strict point-maximizing formulas.</li>
  </ul>

